  "query_preview": "..Isolated to scattered activity remains mostly east\nof I-17 and south of I-40, and hi-res guidance suggests this\nsoutheastern portion of our CWA to be the center of attention for\nshowers/storms through the rest of the overnight period. The\nsevere threat appears to have dwindled, but there's plenty of\nlightning and brief heavy showers still out there. A Flash Flood\nWatch continues through 5 AM MST for Yavapai County, northern Gila\nCounty, and Oak Creek and Sycamore Canyons. \n\nAn additional Flash Flood Watch is in place starting at 2 PM MST \ntoday (Sunday) for all of central/northern AZ and continues \nthrough late Monday night. Monsoonal moisture hangs on tight  \ntoday and much of Monday. This is reflected in our PWAT values \nranging from an inch towards the AZ/UT border and near 1.5 inches \nacross the Mogollon Rim southward. Taking a look at the recent U \nof A WRF run, activity this afternoon could be slightly delayed \nfrom early morning debris clouds, so expect showers/storms to pick\nup closer to noon-1PM. Initially, we'll see activity fire up \nalong the Rim and northward, but then the potential for a MCS to \nform south of our CWA later in the evening will lead to \nwidespread showers/storms Sunday night into Monday. \n\nBy Tuesday, we'll see a quick decline in PWATs as a shortwave\ntrough glides across the Great Basin region and pushes moisture\nfurther south. There is still potential for isolated activity\nprimarily across the Kaibab Plateau and White Mountains Tuesday\nthrough Thursday, but overall should be much quieter in terms of\nactive weather for the middle of the week. Not to mention the\nwestward trajectory of Tropical Storm Karina will not help bring\nin any additional moisture during this timeframe. \n\nFriday onward we could return to an active monsoon pattern. Model\nguidance suggests high pressure strengthening across the central \nUS and another tropical system funneling up the western California\nBaja Coast. There's still uncertainty on how everything will pan \nout, but for now know we could see a quick return of active \nweather Friday and over the upcoming weekend.",
